\description{
Logistic, Poisson, Gaussian and Gamma regression fitted by iteratively
reweighted least squares, which is Fisher scoring for these links.
}
\details{
This is a self-contained reimplementation --- it does not call
\code{stats::glm} --- verified against it to \eqn{10^{-12}} on
coefficients, standard errors, Wald statistics, deviances and AIC for
all four families.

Two details are worth knowing, because they are where a reimplementation
usually drifts from the reference.

First, the standard errors come from the weighted cross-product of the
FINAL IRLS solve, whose weights are evaluated at the \eqn{\eta} that
produced \eqn{\hat\beta} --- not recomputed at the converged
\eqn{\eta}.  The two differ by one Fisher-scoring step: invisible in
\eqn{\hat\beta}, but it moves every standard error in its eighth
digit.  \code{summary.glm} inverts the QR stored by that last step, so
this is what agreement with R means.

Second, the dispersion is fixed at 1 for \code{binomial} and
\code{poisson}, making the Wald statistic normal; for \code{gaussian}
and \code{gamma} it is estimated by the Pearson statistic over the
residual degrees of freedom and the statistic is \eqn{t}, exactly as
\code{summary.glm} does.

Deviance residuals, not raw residuals, are what to plot for a
non-normal family: their sum of squares is the deviance, and they are
roughly symmetric where \eqn{y - \hat\mu} is not.

The same functions exist in the Python package as
\code{morie.fn._glm_core}.
}
